    "Hachiro Arita"
   ],
   "start_page": 282,
   "summary": "This concluding section of the Embassy's report for June 1940 documents U.S.-Japanese diplomatic interactions regarding Shanghai, protests over repeated Japanese air raids on civilian areas in Chungking, the legal proceedings against consular interpreter Matsuo in Taiwan, and reaction to anticipated American export controls on war materials. It covers Japan's relations with other powers, including the signing of the Tientsin agreements with Great Britain, Japanese demands for closing the Burma and Hong Kong supply routes to Chungking, border demarcation with the Soviet Union, trade demands on the Netherlands East Indies, and transit restrictions in French Indochina. Domestically, European developments stimulated strong political demands for closer ties with Germany and Italy, precipitating Prince Konoye's resignation from the Privy Council and Foreign Minister Arita's broadcast proposing regional spheres of influence. The file concludes with surveys of Japanese economic and financial conditions and comprehensive chronological registers of all telegrams and despatches sent to the State Department in June 1940.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of June 1940",
   "to": "Department of State"
  },
