   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Report No. 166 reviewing Japanese developments in July 1940, notably conversations with Foreign Ministers Arita and Matsuoka on future commercial policies, the Open Door, and American support for Chiang Kai-shek. Bilateral relations were aggravated by a physical clash between U.S. Marines and Japanese gendarmes in Shanghai, the arrest of three American yachtsmen at Keelung, continued bombings near American mission properties and gunboats in Chungking, and Japanese concern over U.S. export licensing of petroleum and scrap iron. The report examines Great Britain's three-month suspension of transit traffic over the Burma Road, the arrest of British subjects and the fatal fall of Reuters correspondent Melville Cox while in gendarmerie custody, trade talks with the Netherlands East Indies, and air transport arrangements in French Indochina. Domestically, the section traces the political crisis surrounding Arita's foreign policy address and army dissatisfaction that caused the resignation of the Yonai Cabinet to make way for a new administration under Prince Konoye.",
   "title": "Despatch No. 4959 Transmitting Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of July 1940 (Report No. 166)",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
